WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.740
A Tesla Robotaxi in Austin pushed through ballards after repeated stops, testing claims of an impeccable unsupervised record. This is Tesla Shorts Time, episode 577. An Austin Robotaxi repeatedly stopped, then advanced into plastic ballards in a closed lane, despite detecting the obstacle. The event occurred weeks after Tesla's VP of AI cited zero notable incidents across 380,000 unsupervised miles.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:36.100
No injuries resulted from the contact. The vehicle was operating in a supervised test area when the incident happened.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:45.380
Footage showed the car creeping forward multiple times before finally pushing through the markers. Tesla has not issued a statement on the specific event.

00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:52.840
Now, one thing worth watching is how this kind of edge case sits against broader claims about unsupervised performance.

00:00:52.840 --> 00:01:06.380
The Austin incident showed the vehicle detecting ballards yet still advancing after multiple stops. No injuries resulted, but the event highlights ongoing questions about unsupervised decision logic in real conditions.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:14.760
The Robotaxi was operating in a closed test lane when the contact occurred. Footage shows repeated hesitation before the final push.

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:25.200
While the Robotaxi program faces these edge case questions, Tesla's energy storage side is seeing concrete supply chain progress that could affect deployment speed.

00:01:25.860 --> 00:01:40.480
LG Energy Solution has started cell production at its $2 billion Lansing Michigan facility. The plant will supply LFP cells for Tesla MegaPak 3 units from 2027 under a $4.3 billion supply agreement.

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:54.240
It will also make NMC cells for Toyota EVs. Output is ramping toward 35 gigawatt hours annually. The site currently employs 900 people with plans to reach 1,700 at full capacity.

00:01:54.940 --> 00:02:01.200
Those Michigan cells are explicitly referenced in a new regional power plan that sets large storage targets.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:12.240
The Northwest Power and Conservation Council draft plan calls for 9 gigawatts of renewables, 2.1 gigawatts of natural gas, and 5.2 gigawatts of storage.

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:21.560
It estimates $2.3 billion in fixed costs by 2032. The plan favors renewables plus batteries over new gas were feasible.

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:30.060
It explicitly references Tesla MegaPak systems for utility scale needs. Data centers are highlighted as a primary load driver.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:44.760
Fixed cost projections assume steady deployment of battery resources through the decade. Battery supply agreements like the $4.3 billion LG deal for MegaPak cells expose a core constraint in Tesla Energy's growth path.

00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:51.900
Cell manufacturing remains the largest single-cost driver even after vertical integration in packs and inverters.

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:57.900
Domestic plants must still clear yield and scale hurdles before they fully displace imported supply.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:11.380
When those plants reach 35 gigawatt hours annual output, the marginal costs per stored kilowatt-hour drops, but only if the cells meet the same cycle life and thermal specs already proven in fielded MegaPak's.

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:23.040
The Lansing and Delta Township facilities together represent a deliberate bet on North American cell production to serve both Tesla's own storage products and third-party customers such as Toyota.

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:33.320
Any delay in reaching nameplate capacity directly affects how quickly Tesla can bid on utility scale projects that reference MegaPak by name in their resource plans.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:43.900
The alternative is continued reliance on overseas cell makers, which carries tariff, logistics, and policy risk that a Texas or Michigan facility can sidestep.

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:49.600
Yet building that capacity requires years of capex before the first hour of margin appears.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:58.320
The Northwest Power Plan's 5.2 gigawatt storage target shows how quickly utility demand can outpace any single supplier's ramp.

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:07.040
Tesla therefore faces a straight trade-off between locking in long-term domestic cost curves and accepting slower near-term deployment velocity.

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:14.400
The $2.3 billion fixed cost estimate in the draft plan assumes storage will be available on schedule.

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:23.440
Slipage at either LG site would force utilities to revisit the renewables plus batteries preference and potentially increase the gas component.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:31.860
That tension sits at the center of whether Tesla energy can keep pace with the data center load growth the plan projects through 2032.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:39.000
With storage demand rising quickly, the CyberCab program is also moving forward on its own infrastructure needs.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:45.380
Gold-colored CyberCab units have been spotted in Philadelphia and other states during validation runs.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:51.380
A Starlink antenna has been added for third redundant link alongside 5G and GPS.

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:57.140
Austin charging hub preparations are underway for initial employee rides later this month.

00:04:57.140 --> 00:05:03.000
The integration aims to maintain fleet communication in areas with limited cellular coverage.

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:08.980
Einride confirmed expansion of its Tesla semi-fleet to 500 units for freight and logistics.

00:05:09.640 --> 00:05:12.200
The deployment covers routes across the continent.

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:17.720
The announcement positions the order as one of the largest commercial electric truck rollouts to date.

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:22.700
Einride expects the trucks to begin entering service gradually starting next month.

00:05:22.700 --> 00:05:28.320
The move supports the company's plan to more than double revenue growth in the second half of the year.

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:33.340
On a smaller scale, the CyberTruck program is also moving into review territory.

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:38.600
Tesla Roddy received app access for an upcoming CyberTruck AWD review.

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:42.340
The access enables direct vehicle interaction for testing.

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:48.220
Review preparations include checking performance metrics and software features on the dual motor variant.

00:05:48.820 --> 00:05:51.840
The account shared the update with an image of the app interface.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:56.640
Meanwhile, questions have surfaced about another Texas infrastructure project.

00:05:57.180 --> 00:06:03.120
Observers noted that drawings for the Texas charging hub show V3 cabinets rather than wireless hardware.

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:07.540
This comes despite references to 80 wireless chargers in Phase 2.

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:12.260
The discrepancy questions the accuracy of wireless charging claims for the site.

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:16.840
Community discussion centered on whether the Phase 2 plans had been updated

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:20.080
or if the original description overstated the technology.

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:25.180
The post highlighted the mismatch between the drawing and the stated equipment list.

00:06:25.920 --> 00:06:29.900
CyberCab spotted in Philadelphia at another city to the list of locations

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:33.180
where the two-seater robotaxi has been observed during testing.

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:38.460
The images show the distinctive gold-colored units that have appeared in other test markets.

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:44.060
Footage captured a gold-colored CyberCab with Starlink hardware during public road testing.

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:49.140
The addition marks further evolution in the autonomous two-seaters connectivity setup.

00:06:49.140 --> 00:06:53.260
The vehicle was spotted with the satellite antenna mounted on the roof.

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:59.100
Tesla is preparing a dedicated charging hub in Austin to support the upcoming CyberCab launch.

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:04.820
Employee notifications indicate initial public rollout preparations are underway in the city.

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:10.680
The hub is intended to handle the first employee rides later this month before wider service begins.

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:20.100
Before we go, the next round of validation runs should show whether the added Starlink link changes how the vehicles behave in areas with patchy cellular coverage.

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:23.240
And that's everything worth knowing about Tesla today.

